\description{
\code{coda_multiway()} is the three-way compositional model of Bergeron-Boucher,
Simonacci, Oeppen and Gallo (2018): a Tucker3 decomposition of the centred
log-ratios of the life-table deaths of several populations, with time
indexes shared by all of them. The strata are the populations (for example
the regions of a country). Status: published, with the choices listed
below where the paper leaves the method open. Section and equation numbers
refer to that paper. The model is experimental: compare it with
\code{\link[=coda]{coda()}} (\code{coherent = TRUE}) and \code{\link[=li_lee]{li_lee()}} in a backtest before using it.
}
\details{
\strong{Model (Section 2.2, eq. 5).} For population \code{i}:
\enumerate{
\item the death rates of each year give life-table deaths \code{d[x, t, i]} with
radix 1 at the first age (\code{\link[=life_table]{life_table()}} conventions), as in \code{\link[=coda]{coda()}};
\item \code{a[x, i]} is the geometric mean of \code{d[x, t, i]} over the years, closed
to 1;
\item the deaths are centred by perturbation (\code{d[x, t, i] / a[x, i]}, closed)
and transformed by the centred log-ratio (clr), eq. (3);
\item the array of centred log-ratios \verb{[age, year, population]} follows the
Tucker3 model \verb{clr(d[x, t, i] / a[x, i]) = sum_pqr g[p, q, r] b[x, p] k[t, q] gamma[i, r]}, with age patterns \code{b}, time indexes \code{k} and
population markers \code{gamma} (the paper's beta, kappa and gamma), each of
unit length, and the core array \code{g}; \code{ranks = c(P, Q, R)} gives the
numbers of components of age, time and population. The default
\code{c(1, 1, 1)} is the model of the paper's forecasts (Sections 4 and 5);
\item the time index follows a random walk with drift (\code{order = c(0, 1, 0)},
\code{drift = TRUE}), the ARIMA model that the paper selected by AIC for the
index of Canadian women (Section 5);
\item forecasts are transformed back (inverse clr, perturbation by \code{a[x, i]},
closure) to life-table deaths and then to death rates.
}

With one component per mode, population \code{i} changes by \verb{g gamma[i] b[x] k[t]}: all populations follow one index and one age pattern, at a pace set
by their markers. The forecasts are coherent in this sense; populations
with different markers keep changing at different paces (Section 5.1).
With one population, the fit and the point forecasts are those of
\code{\link[=coda]{coda()}} with the same index model and jump-off; the draws of \code{\link[=coda]{coda()}}
also carry the uncertainty of the estimates.

\strong{Estimation (Sections 2.2 and 4).} Least squares by alternating least
squares: higher-order orthogonal iteration (De Lathauwer, De Moor and
Vandewalle 2000), started from the higher-order singular value
decomposition. The paper uses the Tucker3 model for compositional data of
Gallo (2015), an alternating least-squares algorithm for the same
criterion. The clr of a composition sums to zero and the centring removes
the mean over the years, so \code{b} and \code{k} sum to zero. Signs: each time
index increases over the fitting period, each set of population markers
has a positive sum, and the largest core element of each age pattern is
positive; with one component, life-table deaths then move from ages with
negative \code{b} to ages with positive \code{b} as \code{k} rises. \code{fit$fits$fit_pct}
is the share of the sum of squares of the centred array that the model
explains (the explained variance of Section 4.1), \code{fit$fits$core_pct} the
share of each core element.

\strong{Draws (Section 5).} The paper draws its prediction intervals from
simulations of the time index with resampled errors. Here each time index
is simulated from its ARIMA model with innovations resampled from its
residuals; \code{a}, \code{b}, \code{gamma}, \code{g} and the drift are fixed, and the draws of
the index are shared by all populations. Draws are expected rates;
predictive draws (\code{mt_forecast(predictive = TRUE)}) add Poisson variation
of the deaths given the rates and the exposures, and no other observation
noise.

\strong{Choices where the paper is silent.}
\itemize{
\item Jump-off: the paper states no adjustment, so forecasts start from the
fitted deaths (\code{jump_off = "fitted"}). \code{jump_off = "observed"} starts
each population from its observed deaths of the last year, perturbed by
the change of the model, as in \code{\link[=coda]{coda()}} (Bergeron-Boucher, Canudas-Romo,
Oeppen and Vaupel 2017, Appendix G).
\item Several time indexes (\code{ranks[2] > 1}): the paper forecasts with one
index. Each index then has its own ARIMA model, independent of the
others, as for several components of a CoDa model in Shang and Haberman
(2020).
\item Other ARIMA orders (\code{order}, \code{drift}): the point forecast of an
ARIMA(0,1,q) index with drift gets the first-step correction of \code{\link[=coda]{coda()}}
(Bergeron-Boucher et al. 2017, eq. 20); its draws are shifted by the
same amount.
\item Old ages and zero deaths, as in \code{\link[=coda]{coda()}}: with single-year age groups
from 80 (\code{open = "auto"} or \code{"kannisto"}), the rates from age 80 come
from a Kannisto model fitted per year and population and extended to age
120 (Bergeron-Boucher et al. 2017, Appendix C); with other age groups,
the log death rate of the open interval of each population is regressed
on the time indexes (package-specific). Zero deaths are replaced
multiplicatively (Bergeron-Boucher et al. 2017, Appendix C). The paper
uses five-year age groups to 100+ and five-year periods and combines the
smallest populations to avoid zero counts; the models of this package
need single calendar years.
\item Strata of a hierarchy (see \code{\link[=mt_aggregate_strata]{mt_aggregate_strata()}}) enter the
decomposition as populations of their own; \code{\link[=reconciled]{reconciled()}} makes their
sums coherent.
}

The paper chooses the numbers of components by the DifFit criterion
(Timmerman and Kiers 2000), gives bootstrap confidence intervals of the
parameters (Kiers 2004) and compares models by the AIC of Poisson
deviances (Table 2); these are not implemented. It fits women and men
separately, as \code{stratified("sex", female = coda_multiway(), male = coda_multiway())} does.
}
